|chapter=4|title=Pulling the Strings
|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL16814587W
|passage=A turban and loincloth soaked in blood had been found; also a staff. These '''properties''' were known to have belonged to a toddy drawer. He had disappeared.}}
# {{senseid|en|real property}} A piece of [[real estate]], such as a parcel of land.
#: {{syn|en|land|parcel}}
#: {{ux|en|There is a large house on the '''property'''.}}
#: {{ux|en| He owns over ten '''properties''' in the country.}}
# {{lb|en|British}} [[real estate|Real estate]]; the business of selling [[house]]s.
#: {{ux|en|He works in '''property''' as a housing consultant.}}
# The [[exclusive]] [[right]] of [[possess]]ing, [[enjoy]]ing and disposing of a [[thing]].
# {{senseid|en|object attribute}}An [[attribute]] or [[abstract]] [[quality]] associated with an individual, object or concept.
#: {{ux|en|Charm is his most endearing '''property'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/philip-j-bushnell Philip J. Bushnell]|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=Solvents, Ethanol, Car Crashes & Tolerance|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/solvents-ethanol-car-crashes-amp-tolerance|passage=Furthermore, this increase in risk is comparable to the risk of death from leukemia after long-term exposure to benzene, another solvent, which has the well-known '''property''' of causing this type of cancer.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170618024743/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/solvents-ethanol-car-crashes-amp-tolerance|archivedate=18 June 2017}}
# {{senseid|en|class attribute}}An [[attribute]] or [[abstract]] [[quality]] which is characteristic of a class of objects.
#: {{ux|en|Matter can have many '''properties''', including color, mass and density.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/lee-s-langston Lee S. Langston]|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=The Adaptable Gas Turbine|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/the-adaptable-gas-turbine|passage=Turbines have been around for a long time—windmills and water wheels are early examples. The name comes from the Latin ''turbo'', meaning ''vortex'', and thus the defining '''property''' of a turbine is that a fluid or gas turns the blades of a rotor, which is attached to a shaft that can perform useful work.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130907101447/https://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/4/the-adaptable-gas-turbine|archivedate=7 September 2013}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} An [[editable]] or [[read-only]] [[parameter]] associated with an [[application]], component or [[class]]; especially {{lb|en|OOP}} one that [[encapsulate]]s an underlying [[variable]].
#: {{ux|en|You need to set the debugging '''property''' to "verbose".}}
# {{lb|en|usually|in the plural|theater}} A [[prop]], an object used in a [[dramatic]] [[production]].
#: {{syn|en|prop}}
#: {{ux|en|Costumes and scenery are distinguished from '''property''' properly speaking.}}
